git commit -m "FEATURE: upload a mapping document, review what it changes, then apply it. New MappingProposal model + mapping_ingest_service + /api/mapping-proposals router + Mapping Documents page. Flow: upload -> parse every sheet -> classify each row against the learning library as new / unchanged / conflict -> reviewer approves or rejects the contradictions -> apply writes the approved rows and re-runs the learning pass over EXISTING conversions for that client and object, so a document corrects work already in flight rather than only future conversions. Nothing reaches the library on upload, and apply is refused while any conflict is still undecided - contradictions are never applied silently. An approved conflict REPLACES the existing learning in place rather than adding a rival, otherwise both survive and the mapper arbitrates between them later, which is the ambiguity the review step exists to remove. LAYOUT DETECTION is deterministic first and AI only for sheets it cannot resolve; the model is asked ONLY to identify which column plays which role, never to invent a mapping, so every row still comes verbatim from the document. Two bugs caught by testing against the real NextPower documents before shipping: (1) header detection counted NaN cells as content because str(nan) is the non-empty string 'nan', so every row looked full-width and the title row above the real headers won on the earliest-row tiebreak - all four sheets that previously failed now resolve deterministically, no AI needed (Supplier v3 140 rows, NetSuite 194, Arena eBOS 35, SyteLine 18, matching an independent count exactly); (2) mapping cells are PROSE, not column names - analysts write 'Country / Country Code' and 'Entity Type (transformation needed: e.g. Business -> Corporation)' - and compared literally the supplier document raised 57 conflicts of which every single one was this artefact. Cells are now reduced to the columns they actually offer and matched on ANY alternative, which cut it to 8 genuine contradictions. The bare slash is deliberately left alone because real columns contain one ('Billing State/Province' is one NetSuite column, not two). The 8 that remain are real, notably Supplier Site: the document says Address Label (NetSuite) while the library says city with a 'BU ' prefix (eBOS) - both correct for their own source, which is exactly the call a human should arbitrate. Also adds the missing ClientsApi shape fix on the new page.

The analyst mapping document is now AUTHORITATIVE: nothing is sourced from a column it does not sanction. The deterministic matcher and the AI kept inventing plausible-but-wrong pairs, and because a 'suggested' mapping still exports unless a human rejects it by hand, those guesses reached the FBDI. Observed on a real deployed eBOS vendor run: fax_num -> Account Currency Code, bank_code -> Fax Area Code, country -> Fax Country Code, fax_num -> Customer Number, vend_num -> ATTRIBUTE_NUMBER1, tax_code1 -> Use Withholding Tax, bank_code -> Payee Bank Account Assignment Identifier. A fax number in a currency column fails the load outright. New pass in apply_learned_to_conversion builds an allow-list of every (target field, source column) pair the learning library holds for the object - the seeded analyst docs, transform rules and gold-derived mappings - and sets any still-'suggested' mapping outside that list to not_applicable, so it exports BLANK rather than WRONG. Deliberately untouched: mappings with no source column (constants and control defaults like Batch ID / Import Action), anything a human set (overridden/approved/rejected), and objects holding fewer than 5 learned pairs, which cannot constitute a document - a brand-new object with no doc must still map freely or the tool would produce nothing for it. Replayed against the 54 mappings the deployed app actually produced: 12 unsanctioned guesses dropped, 42 kept. Critically 'Supplier Banks / Supplier Number <- vend_num' is KEPT because it IS in the document - a blanket 'drop everything suggested' would have blanked a required key field, which is why this is an allow-list and not a status filter. Also adds the missing `logger` binding in learning_service (the module logged without ever importing logging).

Two mapping fixes found by running a real new project against the deployed app (158-row eBOS vendor extract, 6 supplier objects). (A) REQUIRED FIELD SHIPPED EMPTY. The normalized target-field fallback dropped any key that two learned spellings collided on, meaning to be cautious. The library holds BOTH 'Supplier Site*' <- city (PREFIX 'BU ') from the analyst doc AND 'Supplier Site' <- 'Address Label' from an older doc; both normalize to 'suppliersite', so the key was discarded, exact match then found only the Address Label row whose source column does not exist in an eBOS extract, and Supplier Site - a REQUIRED Oracle field - generated blank for all 158 rows. A collision there is almost always two spellings of the SAME field (Oracle decorates headers inconsistently across docs), not two different fields, so candidates are now MERGED rather than dropped: safe because the apply loop already discards candidates whose source column is absent from the dataset, and the transform sort puts deliberate rules ahead of plain aliases. Confirmed the same rule already works where there is no collision - Supplier Site Assignment generated 'BU Hayward', 'BU Dallas', 'BU Union City', matching gold. (B) REPLACE / REMOVE_SPECIAL_CHARS / MAP_BOOLEAN / CONDITIONAL_DATE were missing from _STRONG_TRANSFORMS, so a deliberate rule sorted BELOW a plain alias for the same field and lost. Live effect: the analyst rule 'Taxpayer ID <- tax_id, strip spaces' never applied and the FBDI shipped '7 5 -2 1 1 0 3 5 7' instead of '75-2110357' (Tax Registration Number, which had no competing alias, was correct - proving the rule itself was fine). Verified working live in the same run: Business Relationship=SPEND_AUTHORIZED, Client BU/Bill-to BU='NX US BU', Invoice/Payment Currency=USD, Payment Terms<-terms_code (N45/N30), Payment Method<-pay_type, Alternate Name<-name, and the bank *Account Country Code VALUE_MAP emitting US/CN/DE/HK/TH/TW/JP/KR/VN/PL.

FIX: valid sessions were bounced to the sign-in screen on every hard load. useAuth started as {token:null} and relied on App's useEffect(hydrate) to read localStorage - but an effect runs AFTER the first render, and ProtectedRoute renders on that first pass, so it saw token===null and issued <Navigate to='/login' replace> before hydrate could ever run. The token was valid and sitting in localStorage the whole time (confirmed live: a token with 8 hours left, well-formed trinamix.user, backend health 200 from the page, and NO api call attempted before the redirect). Every refresh or deep link therefore looked exactly like an expired session and forced a re-login. authStore now seeds its initial state synchronously from localStorage at store-creation time, so the first render already knows the user is signed in; hydrate() is kept for compatibility. LoginPage also now redirects to / when a token is already present, so landing there from a stale link or a second tab no longer asks for credentials the user already has. Both files typecheck clean.

eBOS/SyteLine supplier mappings from 'eBOS Vendors 04-27-2026', verified against the analyst gold sheet (158 vendors), PLUS a latent learning-engine bug that stopped decorated fields ever matching. (1) LEARNING ENGINE: apply_learned_to_conversion matched the learned target_field to the template field_name by EXACT string, but Oracle decorates headers with asterisks and stray spaces ('Supplier Name*', 'Address Name *', '*Supplier Number', '**Bank Name') while the analyst docs write the plain name - so every decorated field silently never applied, in the column pass, the suppression pass AND the constant-default pass. All three now try the exact key first, then a normalized-name fallback; normalized keys that would collide across two different template fields are dropped so we never guess. This alone activates seeded rows that were already in the library and doing nothing. (2) SEED: 25 eBOS vendor-extract columns added (vend_num, name, city, state, zip, county, country, addr##1-4, phone, fax_num, contact, internal_email_addr, internet_url, terms_code, pay_type, tax_id, bank_name, bank_id, wire_id_acct_num, bank_acct_name) across all 6 supplier objects, with derived rules verified through the real transformation engine: Supplier Site = PREFIX 'BU ' + city (97.4%), Taxpayer ID / Tax Registration Number = tax_id with spaces stripped (98.6/97.1%), Phone = phone with the trailing extension and the leading US '1-' removed, Phone Extension = PHONE_PART (7/7). Alternate Name<-name, Payment Method<-pay_type, Payment Terms<-terms_code, **Bank Name<-bank_name, *Account Number<-wire_id_acct_num, Account Name<-bank_acct_name all 100%. 30 of 39 verifiable rules match gold at >=95%. (3) CONSTANTS (NextPower-scoped): Supplier Type=Standard, Business Relationship=SPEND_AUTHORIZED, Invoice/Payment Currency=USD, Client BU/Bill-to BU='NX US BU' - each confirmed as the single distinct value across all 158 gold rows. Supplier Type=Standard supersedes the generic SUPPLIER control default, which only fills fully-blank columns. (4) CORRECTED three rows the gold disproves: **Branch Name<-Bank ID and IBAN<-WIRE ID were wrong (Bank ID is the routing number, WIRE ID is the account number; gold leaves both targets empty) and Remit Advice Email is empty in all 158 gold rows, so mapping it over-populated the bank file. Also switched the Oracle E-Mail columns from internal_email_addr (the internal AP mailbox, 10.5%) to contact (42.8%). Applies to EXISTING NextPower conversions: the seeders upsert-and-upgrade at startup and generate re-runs apply_learned force=True for every supplier template (all under the 300-field heavy gate), so a Regenerate picks it all up with no re-mapping; user 'overridden'/'rejected' choices are still respected. (5) Found by running a simulated new project over the real 158-row eBOS extract: *Account Country Code was emitting 'USA' from the eBOS country column, because the only seed row for it was the NetSuite one whose source already held a 2-char 'US'. Oracle IBY validates that field as an ISO-3166 alpha-2 territory code, so every bank-account row would have failed import. Added an eBOS row with a VALUE_MAP (USA->US, China->CN, Hong Kong->HK, Taiwan->TW, Japan->JP, Korea->KR, Thailand->TH, Vietnam->VN, Germany->DE, Poland->PL + the common rest); the learning engine sorts transform-carrying rows ahead of plain aliases so it wins. Verified: the generated bank file now emits US/CN/DE/HK/TH/TW/JP/KR/VN/PL. Simulated new-project run over all 6 supplier objects populates 48 fields and agrees with the analyst gold on 4328/4956 populated cells (87.3%). Bundles the earlier rejected-mapping output fix. Reported: reject a suggested mapping, the UI and the downloaded mapping CSV both say 'rejected', but the generated FBDI still carried values from that rejected column. ROOT CAUSE in build_converted_dataframe/_transform_frame: the per-target dedup ranks rejected(1) above suggested(0), so a rejected row correctly WINS and becomes the field's mapping - but the only skip was for not_applicable, and 'rejected' merely suppressed the suggested_transformation (status != 'rejected' on the rules line) while has_src stayed true, so the rejected column's values were still emitted. FIX: a discarded status (not_applicable OR rejected) never reads source_column - has_src now requires 'not _discarded' - and such a mapping is skipped entirely unless an explicit default_value was attached, in which case that default is emitted as a CONSTANT (preserves Invoice Match Option = Receipt). Verified with the real _transform_frame: approved keeps its source, rejected emits no column, rejected+default emits the constant, not_applicable still skipped, and the rejected column's values no longer appear anywhere. Works for EXISTING rejections too: this is a generate-time fix that reads the stored status, apply_learned _eligible() never re-applies a 'rejected' row (suggested, or approved under force, only), and the dedup keeps rejected above a stale suggested row - so previously-rejected mappings are honoured on the next Generate with no re-mapping. Also bundles the upload durable-copy logging hardening."
